content.text:
Add Convex to an existing nextjs-app-router project by installing the Convex package, initializing or reusing the convex/ backend directory, and applying framework-specific client provider wiring.

Install/init commands:
  pnpm add convex
  if [ -n "$CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY" ] || { grep -qs "^CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=" [REDACTED] && ! grep -qs "^CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=anonymous" [REDACTED]; }; then npx convex dev; elif [ -f "$HOME/.convex/config.json" ]; then npx convex dev --configure new --dev-deployment cloud --project "$(basename "$PWD")"; else CONVEX_AGENT_MODE=anonymous npx convex dev; fi

### app/ConvexClientProvider.tsx
```typescript
"use client";

import { ConvexProvider, ConvexReactClient } from "convex/react";
import { ReactNode } from "react";

const convex = new [REDACTED]!);

export function ConvexClientProvider({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  return <ConvexProvider client={convex}>{children}</ConvexProvider>;
}
```

### app/layout.tsx
```typescript
import { ConvexClientProvider } from "./ConvexClientProvider";

export const metadata = {
  title: "My App",
};

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <ConvexClientProvider>{children}</ConvexClientProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

Repo checks:
- Confirm package.json has the convex package installed.
- Confirm a convex/ directory exists before adding backend functions.
- Confirm the frontend is wrapped in ConvexProvider before using useQuery or useMutation.
- Confirm the public Convex URL environment variable is available before the frontend dev server starts.
- Run the framework dev server and verify a small useQuery call renders without provider or URL errors.

Notes:
- NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL must be set before the framework dev server boots. Otherwise `ConvexReactClient(undefined)` throws at module load.
- Convex's `npx convex dev` writes `CONVEX_URL` to [REDACTED]`. Next.js requires the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix — bridge it (export NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=$CONVEX_URL or update [REDACTED]`) before starting `next dev`.
- Provider must be a Client Component (`"use client"`). The root layout stays a Server Component and wraps `{children}` with `<ConvexClientProvider>`.
- If the user is SIGNED IN to Convex, use a real cloud deployment — always. Anonymous local mode is ONLY for users with no Convex account. Check before starting `npx convex dev`.
- Signed-in signals (any one): `CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY` is set in the environment (deploy-key / CI auth). `~/.convex/config.json` exists — the user has run `npx convex login` (the file is deleted on logout). [REDACTED]` has a `CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=` line whose value does NOT start with `anonymous` — already linked to a real (cloud or account-local) deployment. An `anonymous-…` value does NOT count; it's an unlinked local backend.
- Deploy key set, or a NON-anonymous `CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT` already in [REDACTED]`: plain `npx convex dev` runs against that deployment with no prompt. Don't add `CONVEX_AGENT_MODE=anonymous`.
- Signed in (`~/.convex/config.json` exists) but no real deployment yet — including when only a leftover `CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=anonymous-…` is present: provision cloud with `npx convex dev --configure new --dev-deployment cloud --project <app-slug>` (add `--team <slug>` if the account has more than one team; a single team is auto-selected). Do NOT drop to anonymous local for a signed-in user.
- ONLY when there is no account at all (no deploy key, no `~/.convex/config.json`, no link): `CONVEX_AGENT_MODE=anonymous npx convex dev` — a zero-touch local backend with no login / team / link prompt. This is the sole anonymous case; it also suppresses the "link it now?" prompt that plain `npx convex dev` raises against an anonymous deployment.
- The project already appears to have a Convex backend directory, so focus on client wiring and generated API imports.
- For auth, either add Convex Auth for an all-Convex setup or keep an existing provider such as Clerk/Auth0/WorkOS and configure Convex JWT auth. Do not add a custom session table unless there is a very specific reason.

